.TH TPMD-DEMO 1 "2026" "tpmd" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tpmd-demo \- toy consumer for tpmd
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tpmd-demo
.RI [ \-\-socket " path" ]
.I command
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tpmd-demo
is a toy consumer of
.BR tpmd (1).
It is shipped so that an installed agent can be exercised on a real machine
without writing any code: it provokes the representative successes and the
representative failures, and it prints what came back.
.PP
Its
.B run
subcommand is the exception: it starts an agent of its own, which is a
convenient way to demonstrate the whole life cycle on a development machine but
not a way to talk to the installed service. Against the packaged service, use
the other subcommands with an explicit
.BR \-\-socket .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B probe
Connect without a key and print the capability announcement: what the agent is,
and which security levels it can reach.
.TP
.B provision
Create a key and print its fingerprint.
.TP
.BI "sign " fingerprint " " message
Sign a UTF-8 message with a key and print the signature.
.TP
.BI "describe " fingerprint
Print a key's metadata.
.TP
.BI "destroy " fingerprint
Destroy a key.
.TP
.B status
Print the daemon status for this namespace.
.TP
.B errors
Provoke representative failures and print how each one is reported.
.TP
.B run
Start an agent, walk a whole life cycle against it, and stop it. Development
only.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Endpoint to connect to. Defaults to the per\-user endpoint, so against the
packaged system service this must be given explicitly, for example
.IR /run/tpmd/tpmd.sock .
.TP
.BI \-\-namespace " name"
Namespace to connect as. Defaults to
.BR tpmd\-demo .
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " path"
State directory, used by
.BR run .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tpmd (1)
